Hello Angus and team,

an offer for an independent review, with the framing your contact page asks
for: this is a manual modelling tool, and the test I am proposing involves no
AI at all.

Solidon3D sits between a downloaded model and the slicer. Click a hole and its
dimensions appear on the model itself, type 8 instead of 6 and the countersink
follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check report before you export,
hand the file to the slicer you already use. No CAD knowledge needed.

The test that shows the difference in a few minutes: open any model with a
knurled grip or a honeycomb panel. In a mesh tool that is a few hundred faces
and nothing to edit. In Solidon 0.5.0 it is one feature with pitch, cell width
and depth, removed with a click and reapplied with three numbers. On a
cylinder the pattern is measured in the unrolled surface, so the grooves are
equally deep all the way round and the pattern closes without a seam.

The structural change of this release is that the part stays exact. Holes,
countersinks and threads survive moving, duplicating and removing without
being converted to triangles, and STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly, so
the result can be checked in a CAD system rather than judged by eye. That is
also the gap the product is aimed at: CAD packages accept an STL but cannot
grip triangles, the mesh tools that could edit them are discontinued, and
parametric customisers need an author who anticipated the parameter.

If a print is part of the review, one detail is worth mentioning: clearance is
not entered as a number but as a reference to the material, so "clearance for
PETG" rather than a value in millimetres. Print the supplied tolerance ladder,
measure it, set the profile, and every fit uses your printer's numbers from
then on.

On the AI question, so it is stated plainly rather than discovered later:
there is an assistant in the product. It is optional, it can be switched off,
it can run locally on your own GPU, and no function of the software depends on
it. The review I am asking for is of the modelling tool.

Nothing is needed from me to run the test. The demo is free and complete until
the end of October, and I am happy to send images or the models I used.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, in six languages, with no account
and no network.

